<node id="Attention Mechanisms in Neural Networks (image)">
  <data key="d0">Attention Mechanisms in Neural Networks (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 2 illustrates the concepts of Scaled Dot-Product Attention and Multi-Head Attention, key components of the Transformer architecture. The left side details the process of scaled dot-product attention, while the right side shows multiple attention layers operating in parallel. This visual representation supports the surrounding text's discussion on the encoder and decoder stacks, enhancing understanding of attention mechanisms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6d8393f9437e15137861837ec245e754</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763837951</data>
</node>
<node id="Scaled Dot-Product Attention (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Scaled Dot-Product Attention (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation defines the scaled dot-product attention mechanism, which computes a weighted sum of values based on the compatibility of queries and keys, facilitating effective information retrieval in the Transformer model.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2aee075b8c37555450aa7c2ad4472c4</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763837951</data>
</node>
<node id="Dot Product Variance Explanation (discarded)">
  <data key="d0">Dot Product Variance Explanation (discarded)</data>
  <data key="d1">discarded</data>
  <data key="d2">This content explains the variance of the dot product of query and key vectors in the attention mechanism, highlighting the need for scaling to prevent large values that can affect softmax gradients. It relates to the broader discussion on attention mechanisms in the Transformer model.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-562945b1396f11e4a4ad6121c818612d</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763837951</data>
</node>
